================================================================================
Third-party components
================================================================================

ABFE-IBS vendors no third-party source and redistributes no third-party
binary.  Everything below is imported or linked at run time from a copy the
user installed separately (conda-forge).  This section exists to satisfy the
attribution obligations those components carry.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------
OpenMM -- https://openmm.org
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

OpenMM is NOT distributed under a single license.  Per the OpenMM User Guide,
section 8.1.2:

    "The public API, the low level API, the reference platform, the CPU
     platform, and the application layer are all distributed under the MIT
     license.  The CUDA, HIP, and OpenCL platforms are distributed under the
     GNU Lesser General Public License (LGPL)."

Both halves are in play here:

  * The Python layer imports the MIT-licensed application layer and public
    API.
  * The native plugin under plugins/LocalManyBodyResidual/platforms/cuda/
    includes OpenMM's CUDA platform headers -- CudaContext.h and
    openmm/common/ComputeArray.h -- and so builds against the LGPL half.

ABFE-IBS relies on LGPL-3 section 4(d)(1): the plugin is loaded at run time
by OpenMM's own plugin loader, against a copy of OpenMM that the user
installed separately.  Concretely:

  * No OpenMM source is copied into this repository.
  * No OpenMM binary is redistributed with it.  .gitignore excludes *.so,
    *.a and plugins/*/build*/, so only plugin source is tracked.
  * ABFE-IBS does not modify OpenMM itself; the plugin is a separate shared
    library that registers its own kernel factory.

License texts:
    LGPL-3.0  https://www.gnu.org/licenses/lgpl-3.0.txt
    GPL-3.0   https://www.gnu.org/licenses/gpl-3.0.txt

NOTE -- this repository is a source-only distribution, for which linking to
those texts is sufficient.  If a binary distribution is ever produced that
bundles a compiled plugin alongside OpenMM libraries, the full LGPL-3 and
GPL-3 texts must be shipped inside that distribution.

The plugin's directory layout, build scaffolding and API skeleton are derived
from the OpenMM example plugin, which is MIT-licensed:

    Portions copyright (c) Stanford University and the Authors.


